During the blower door test, we identified that your house has excess air leakage associated with the following locations:



** Around the attic hatch (can seal with removable caulking such as "Draft Attack")



** The ductwork for the furnace is allowing air infiltration, which is likely coming in through the crawlspace ductwork, because the crawlspace is very cold. Duct work in the spare bedroom on the main floor has very cold air coming in through the vents.



** Cold air coming from the ductwork could also be coming from the fresh air supply duct, as it appears there is no damper installed on this duct, allowing cold air to dump into the supply ducts. Direct investigation of this was not possible, but there was a lot of cold air in the furnace duct, in the furnace room, when the furnace wasn't running.



** Around the current fresh air supply pipe and combustion air supply pipe for the furnace. Spray foam could be applied here, as discussed during the audit.



** Around the penetration for the dryer hose. This connection could be sealed up with caulking or spray foam.



** The most significant air leakage is occurring in the crawlspace, causing discomfort on the main floor of the home, and resulting in frozen pipes in the main floor bathroom. This needs further investigation, but please see the "Additional Comments" section for a more in depth discussion about this issue.



Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a minimum of a 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss. This is estimated to save $100 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.2 GJ, and drastically increase the comfort levels of your home.



There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year. There are rebates available for air sealing through the Greener Homes Grant, offered by the Government of Canada.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</airSeal>
                <ceilings>

CEILING INSULATION



The attic space of your home appears to have a total of R28 of insulation, comprised of blown cellulose insulation. I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R60. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting.



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R28 up to a minimum R60, this is estimated to save $140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.6 GJ, and qualify for a $ 125 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ?????????insulation????????? rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. Please note, at the time of writing (December 2021) the EEIP rebate is fully subscribed and Arctic Energy Alliance is currently taking a waitlist. Check in with AEA after April 1st, 2022 to see if circumstances have changed.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



There are also rebates available for attic insulation through the Greener Homes Grant, offered by the Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <windows>

WINDOWS



The windows in this house are a mixture of new windows, and old windows. Most windows are older triple paned, wood framed windows, but there are some new vinyl framed, triple paned windows with a single low E coating. The older window on the 2nd floor, beside the wood stove flue pipe shows evidence of a blown seal.



Upgrading specific windows in this house to ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3 is something that could be considered as an upgrade for this home. If this is something the homeowner wishes to pursue I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



Savings: By replacing all the older windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 190 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.8 GJ. 



There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, however there are rebates available from the Greener Homes Grant - Government of Canada. Please see their website for more details on requirement.



--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------





</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

PROPANE Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric



You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly in the north. You may want to consider replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified, instantaneous, condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 1300 per year in energy costs, slightly increase your annual energy usage by 0.3 GJ,  and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. Please note, at the time of writing (December 2021) the EEIP rebate is fully subscribed and Arctic Energy Alliance is currently taking a waitlist. Check in with AEA after April 1st 2022 to see if circumstances have changed.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

HEATING SYSTEM UPGRADE - PROPANE FURNACE



The main heat source in your house is an older 82% efficient oil furnace. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.



Savings: By replacing your current oil furnace with a 95% AFUE condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 620 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. Please note, at the time of writing (December 2021) the EEIP rebate is fully subscribed and Arctic Energy Alliance is currently taking a waitlist. Check in with AEA after April 1st 2022 to see if circumstances have changed.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentiveprogram



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



CRAWLSPACE



The crawlspace of your home is the largest contributor to the discomfort issues you are experiencing, and should be your first priority when considering retrofits to your home.



Upon investigation of the crawlspace I beleive there is signifcant air leakage occurring down there, most likely located along the headers of the main floor above. As this space is meant to be heated, it is important to seal up all air leakage points in order to trap the heated air in that space. To do this I recommend considering applying spray foam to all header areas, as spray foam will fill all the nooks and crannies, as well as provide a high insulating value. After that is complete, I recommend adding insulation to the few locations on the foundation walls where it was missing. Next make sure to attach the existing poly to the header area. You want a complete seal all the way around the entire perimeter of the crawl space. Keeping this space air tight should make the crawl space a reasonable temperature and result in a warmer floor above it, plus prevent cold air from infiltrating into your heating ductwork. When you completely seal up this space it could cause excess moisture build up in the crawlspace and this will need to be monitored. You can install moisture meters and even hook them up to exhaust fans on automatic settings.



Another thing to consider is installing a damper vent on the crawl space vent opening. It would open when the furnace turns on, but remain closed when the furnace is off. This would prevent cold air infiltration into the ductowork.



You could also consider insulating the crawlspace ductwork with the fibreglass batt insulation by taping it around the ductwork. This would help keep heat in the ductwork on its way to rooms on the main floor.



Another problem to have a contractor tackle would be installing an automatic damper on the fresh air intake of your furnace. Currently it seems (unable to investigate) that either there is no damper installed or the damper is stuck open. The result of this is cold air constantly pouring into the ductwork from outside, and ending up in your mechanical room and your crawlspace. This is the cold air you can feel when standing beside the furnace in the mechanical room. Having a damper installed would allow the furnace to operate as intended, only pulling in cold, fresh air when it turns on to distribute heat to your home.



Summary of steps:



1. Consult contractors about air sealing the header space. Ask about spray foam, and other ideas. Shop around.

2. Patch up existing holes in EPS insulation along the inside of foundation walls.

3. Have contractors investigate other unknowns in the crawlspace walls, focussing on a continuous air and insulation barrier.

4. Ensure the poly (air and vapour barrier) is attached continuously around the entire crawlspace, directly to the newly insulated header space, and to the poly on the dirt floor below, as well as to all water and sewer penetrations.

5. Install a device to monitor moisture levels.

6. If not installing, a new, direct vent, condensing propane furnace, as recommended above, then approach a contractor about installing a damper on your fresh air intake attached to your furnace.

7. If installing a new furnace, ensure it has been installed correctly, including with a damper on the fresh air intake.

8. Consider insulating crawlspace ductwork, and installing a damper on the vent down there.

9. Observe the effect on the comfort level of the main floor bedroom. Further investigation may be required.



------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 3.7 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current oil furnace and your wood stove vent up a chimney and are susceptible to back drafting.



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

 

        - two bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.61 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



